hypotheses.statement:
For every $L<\infty$ there is $C_{q,m,L}<\infty$ such that $$\label{ker:sheet-gain}
 P(c,H)(v)\leq C_{q,m,L}H(v)
 \quad\hbox{for almost every }v,
 \qquad c\in\mathcal S_L.$$ More quantitatively, for $R\geq1$, $$\label{ker:shell-tail}
 \mathcal K_c\bigl(v;
      R<|p|+|\xi-v|\leq2R\bigr)
 \leq C_{q,m,L}R^2e^{-cR^2},$$ where $c>0$ is an absolute constant. The same conclusions, multiplied by $A_0$, hold under sheet domination with amplitude $A_0$.

The construction parameters can be fixed so that every dormant finite evolution and every cap solution with $0<T\le T_0$ continues globally. There are constants $B<\infty$ and $\kappa>0$, independent of $N$, $b_N\ge1$, and $T$, such that $$\label{global:uniform-bound}
 \|w(t)\|_H\le B,\qquad \nu_F^{b_N}(t,x,v)\ge\kappa,
 \qquad t\ge t_1.$$ Every active jet satisfies <a href="#global:cold-decay" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="global:cold-decay">[global:cold-decay]</a> through its retirement, the total retirement increment is bounded by <a href="#global:retirement" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="global:retirement">[global:retirement]</a>, and the hot component satisfies the small bound of <a href="#early:continuation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="early:continuation">24</a> on $[0,t_1]$.

outputs.exact_statement:
For the normalization in <a href="#eq:collision" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:collision">[eq:collision]</a>, the symmetric bilinear gain is $$\label{ker:carleman}
 P(c,h)(v)
 =2\int_{\mathbb R^3}c(p)\,\,\mathrm dp
       \int_{\mathbb R^3}
       \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)h(\xi)\,\,\mathrm d\xi.$$ For fixed $p\ne v$, the inner delta denotes surface measure on the plane $(v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)=0$, divided by $|v-p|$. For incoming velocities $p,\xi$, the outgoing measure has total mass $2\pi|p-\xi|$ and is uniform on the sphere with diameter $[p,\xi]$. Formula <a href="#ker:carleman" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:carleman">[ker:carleman]</a> also defines the gain when $c$ is a nonnegative velocity measure and $h$ is a nonnegative density, whenever the displayed integral is finite almost everywhere.

Let $\lambda$ be a probability mixture of the measures $\mathcal U_d$, $d>0$, and $\delta_0$, and let $\rho$ be any axial probability measure. No bound on its density or its support is required. Put $c=\lambda\otimes\rho$. For $R\geq1$, $\bar v\ne0$, and an interval $I\subset\mathbb R$ of length $0<\ell\leq2R$, one has $$\begin{aligned}
 &\frac{1}{W(v)}\int c(\,\mathrm dp)
   \int_{|\xi-v|\leq R}
    \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)W(\xi)\,\,\mathrm d\xi
     \leq C_q R^2,\label{ker:geometric-total}\\
 &\frac{1}{W(v)}\int c(\,\mathrm dp)
   \int_{\substack{|\xi-v|\leq R\\ \xi_3\in I}}
    \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)W(\xi)\,\,\mathrm d\xi
     \leq C_q\bigl(R\ell+R^{q-1}\ell^{2-q}\bigr).
     \label{ker:geometric-interval}
\end{aligned}$$ The constants are independent of all transverse widths and of $\rho$. In particular, these statements hold with $\lambda=\mathcal G_d$ for every $d\geq0$.

For every $L<\infty$ there is $C_{q,m,L}<\infty$ such that $$\label{ker:sheet-gain}
 P(c,H)(v)\leq C_{q,m,L}H(v)
 \quad\hbox{for almost every }v,
 \qquad c\in\mathcal S_L.$$ More quantitatively, for $R\geq1$, $$\label{ker:shell-tail}
 \mathcal K_c\bigl(v;
      R<|p|+|\xi-v|\leq2R\bigr)
 \leq C_{q,m,L}R^2e^{-cR^2},$$ where $c>0$ is an absolute constant. The same conclusions, multiplied by $A_0$, hold under sheet domination with amplitude $A_0$.

Fix $1\leq L<\infty$. Uniformly for $c\in\mathcal S_L$ and outgoing velocities off the axis, the tails in $|p|+|\xi-v|$ are negligible: $$\label{ker:uniform-tail}
 \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}\mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_v
 \mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\xi-v|>R)=0.$$ Moreover, $$\label{ker:large-output}
 \lim_{M\to\infty}\sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}
 \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{|v|>M}\mathcal K_c(v;\mathbb R^3\times\mathbb R^3)=0.$$ Both conclusions hold under uniformly bounded sheet amplitudes.

Let a measure in parameters $w=(\bar w,w_3)\in\mathbb R^2\times\mathbb R$ have density $j$ satisfying $$0\leq j(w)\leq C_0\pi^{-3/2}e^{-|w|^2}.$$ Let its velocity image be given by $$\label{ker:parameter-map}
 \bar p=d\bar w,\qquad p_3=\Phi(w_3),\qquad
 0\leq d\leq\tfrac1{16},\qquad
 |\Phi(a)|\leq A+\delta|a|,\quad0\leq\delta\leq\tfrac1{16},$$ where $\Phi$ is merely measurable. The image measure is dominated by $C_0$ times a sheet in $\mathcal S_{L_A}$, with $L_A$ depending only on $A$. Its gain satisfies Lemma <a href="#lem:sheet-bound" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:sheet-bound">6</a> with a constant depending only on $q,m,A,C_0$.*


*If $c_{>B}$ is the image of the restricted parameter measure $\mathbf 1_{|w|>B}j(w)\,\,\mathrm dw$, then $$\label{ker:parameter-tail}
 \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_v\frac{P(c_{>B},H)(v)}{H(v)}
 \leq C_{q,m,A,C_0}e^{-B^2/2},\qquad B\geq0.$$ Thus parameter truncations are valid in the normalized collision measure, even when $\Phi$ compresses intervals or has atoms in its image law. Probability mixtures of measurable families with common $q,m,A,C_0$ and the width bounds in <a href="#ker:parameter-map" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:parameter-map">[ker:parameter-map]</a> preserve these assertions. More generally, a positive mixing measure of total mass $B_0<\infty$ multiplies the domination amplitude and quantitative upper bounds by $B_0$; the sheet parameter $L_A$ is unchanged. For <a href="#ker:parameter-tail" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:parameter-tail">[ker:parameter-tail]</a>, impose the restriction $|w|>B$ in each component before mixing. Additional measurable masks taking values in $[0,1]$ preserve the assertions.

There is a constant $C_{q,m}$ such that $$\label{ker:hot-gain}
 P(H,H)\leq C_{q,m}H
 \quad\hbox{almost everywhere}.$$ In fact the proof below works for every $m>5$.

For each $0\leq B<\infty$ and $\varepsilon>0$ there is a finite constant $C_{B,\varepsilon}$ such that every measurable density $j$ with $0\leq j\leq BH$ satisfies $$\label{ker:gain-small-mass}
 P(H,j)(v)\leq H(v)
 \left(\varepsilon+C_{B,\varepsilon}
                         \int_{\mathbb R^3}j(p)\,\,\mathrm dp\right)
 \qquad\hbox{for almost every }v.$$ The same constant works with $P^b$ in place of $P$.

A fixed sufficiently large $C_m$ gives, for $0\le t\le L_s$, $$\begin{aligned}
 f_i&\le m_i,\qquad
 D_tm_i\ge \frac1{L_s}
       \left(1+\frac78\frac{|\xi|^2}{d_s^2}\right)m_i,
 \label{cold:barrier-derivative}\\
 P(m_i,m_i)&=m_i\nu_{m_i}
       \le C\frac K{s}(d_s+|\xi|)m_i.
 \label{cold:barrier-gain}
\end{aligned}$$ The differential inequality holds almost everywhere along flights. For sufficiently small $s_0$, $f_i(t,x,v)>0$ implies $x\in\mathcal O_i(t)$ throughout the same time interval.

There are $t_1\in(0,1/32]$, $\beta'>4$, and constants $C,c>0$, independent of $\delta$, $R_\mu$, and $s_0$, such that every finite evolution of <a href="#con:colors" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="con:colors">[con:colors]</a> satisfies, on its interval of existence, $$\label{early:bath-bound}
 0\le u(t,x,v)\le C e^{-\beta'|v|^2}
       \exp\!\left(-\frac{cD_0(x)^2}{t}\right),
 \qquad 0<t\le t_1,
 \qquad D_0(x)=\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,D_{\rm hole}^c).$$ For sufficiently small $s_0$ depending on $\delta$, one also has $$\label{early:hole-distance}
 D_0(x)\ge\delta
 \qquad (0\le t\le t_1,\ x\in\mathcal O(t)).$$

For fixed $\delta>0$, there are $\bar s_0,T_0>0$, uniform in $N$ and the collision cutoff, with the following property. For each $0<s_0\le\bar s_0$, each $0<T\le T_0$, and each finite approximation, a seed parameter $\lambda\in(0,L_0)$ gives a solution on $[0,T]$ satisfying $$\label{early:cap}
 Z(t)\le m_T(t+s_N)\quad(0\le t\le T),
 \qquad
 \max_{0\le t\le T}\frac{Z(t)}{m_T(t+s_N)}=1.$$ The dormant solution exists on a fixed initial interval and satisfies $$\label{early:dormant-bound}
 Z(t)\le C'_{A,\delta}t^A.$$ The interval and constant in <a href="#early:dormant-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="early:dormant-bound">[early:dormant-bound]</a> are independent of $N$, the collision cutoff, and sufficiently small $s_0$.

Let $A:[0,\infty)\times(\mathbb R^3/\Lambda)\to[0,\infty)$ be measurable, write $A_s=A(s,\cdot)$, and suppose $$\sup_s\|A_s\|_\infty\le A_*,\qquad
 \sup_s\|A_s\|_1\le a_*.$$ For a fixed bounded velocity set $V$ and $\rho>0$, $$\label{global:ball-average}
 \int_V A_s(x-\tau v)\,\mathrm dv
 \le C_{V,\rho}a_*
 \qquad(\tau\ge\rho),$$ uniformly in $x,s,\tau$. If $A_*$ is fixed, the following quantities tend to zero as $a_*\to0$: $$\begin{aligned}
 &\sup_{t\ge t_1,x}
 \int_{t_1}^t e^{-\kappa(t-s)}
       \int H(v)A_s(x-(t-s)v)\,\mathrm dv\,\mathrm ds,
       \label{global:damped-average}\\
 &\sup_{t\ge t_1,x}
 \int_0^{t_1}\int H(v)A_s(x-(t-s)v)\,\mathrm dv\,\mathrm ds,
       \label{global:early-average}
\end{aligned}$$ where $\kappa>0$ and $t_1>0$ are fixed. The supremum in $x$ is essential.

Let $B>B_0+2$ be fixed and suppose <a href="#global:bootstrap" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="global:bootstrap">[global:bootstrap]</a> holds on an interval beginning at $t_1$. After taking $\varepsilon_E$, $\zeta$, and $s_0$ sufficiently small depending on $B$, one has on that interval $$\label{global:gain-bound}
 P^b(w,w)\le C_5H,
 \qquad C_5:=\|P(\mu,\mu)\|_H+1.$$ In particular, $C_5$ is independent of $B$, the approximation, and the cap horizon. The estimate holds also at the appropriate one-sided sections of retirement times.

The construction parameters can be fixed so that every dormant finite evolution and every cap solution with $0<T\le T_0$ continues globally. There are constants $B<\infty$ and $\kappa>0$, independent of $N$, $b_N\ge1$, and $T$, such that $$\label{global:uniform-bound}
 \|w(t)\|_H\le B,\qquad \nu_F^{b_N}(t,x,v)\ge\kappa,
 \qquad t\ge t_1.$$ Every active jet satisfies <a href="#global:cold-decay" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="global:cold-decay">[global:cold-decay]</a> through its retirement, the total retirement increment is bounded by <a href="#global:retirement" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="global:retirement">[global:retirement]</a>, and the hot component satisfies the small bound of <a href="#early:continuation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="early:continuation">24</a> on $[0,t_1]$.

Every sequence <a href="#eq:rescaling" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:rescaling">[eq:rescaling]</a> has a subsequence for which $h_{r_N}\rightharpoonup g$ in local spacetime $L^1$ on $(0,U)\times\mathbb R^3_x\times\mathbb R^3_v$, where $$\label{eq:limit-equation}
0\leq g(t,x,v)\leq(t+l)H(v),\qquad
D_tg=2P(B,g)-g\nu_B.$$ Here $B$ is <a href="#eq:background" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:background">[eq:background]</a>; its gain and loss are defined by integration against the velocity measure. Both terms on the right are locally integrable, with a velocity majorant uniform in position on each compact positive-time interval. Compactly supported velocity averages of $h_{r_N}$ converge strongly in local $L^1_{t,x}$. The limit has a locally strongly continuous $L^1$ representative at positive times and obeys the integrating-factor formula <a href="#dil:mild" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="dil:mild">[dil:mild]</a> below.*


*If $l>0$, pass also to a limit $\lambda_N\to\lambda_0$ of the seed parameters. The conclusion and its local continuity extend to time zero, with $$\label{dil:initial-trace}
g(0,x,v)=\lambda_0 l\,\chi(x/l,v).$$

There are nonnegative velocity measures $A_n(t,x,\,\mathrm dp)$ and numbers $\varepsilon_n\to0$ with the following properties. After discarding finitely many terms, the measures $A_n$ are defined from free transport on the whole interval $0\le t\le1$, independently of the cap interval $[0,U_n]$. The bounds on $A_n$ below hold on that whole interval; comparison with $h_n$ is used only while the cap bound holds.*


1.  *On $0<t<\min(1,U_n)$, along almost every free characteristic, $$\label{nc:positive-equation}
     D_th_n\le 2P(A_n,h_n)+\varepsilon_n H .$$ At each point $A_n$ contains at most one main free core. Its dilated scale $\varrho=s/r_n$ satisfies $|y|\asymp\varrho$, with coordinates measured from its periodic copy, and $$\frac{2P(A_n,H)}{H}\le\frac{C}{t+\ell_n}.$$*


2.  *Before spatial masks are imposed, a core of scale $\varrho=s/r_n$ and sign $\sigma$ is the pushforward of $$\label{nc:core-parameters}
     \frac{K S\bigl(\gamma(z-tp_3)/\varrho\bigr)}
          {\varrho+t\alpha\gamma S\bigl(\gamma(z-tp_3)/\varrho\bigr)}
     J(w)\,\mathrm dw ,
     \qquad
     \bar p=d_s\bar w,\quad
     p_3=\sigma+\alpha\tanh\bigl(\gamma(z-tp_3)/\varrho\bigr)+d_sw_3 .$$ The actual masks are measurable factors between zero and one. The density in <a href="#nc:core-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="nc:core-parameters">[nc:core-parameters]</a> is at most $C(t+\varrho)^{-1}J(w)$.*


3.  *For $t\ge a>0$, the normalized gain measure $$\label{nc:normalized-kernel}
     \mathcal K_n(t,x,v;\,\mathrm dp\,\,\mathrm d\xi)
     =
     4A_n(t,x,\,\mathrm dp)\,
     \delta\bigl((v-p)\cdot(\xi-v)\bigr)
     \frac{H(\xi)}{H(v)}\,\mathrm d\xi$$ has total mass at most $C/a$. Uniformly in its base point, the contributions from large $|p|+|\xi-v|$, or from large retained Gaussian parameters in a product-sheet majorant, tend to zero. Its total mass tends uniformly to zero as $|v|\to\infty$.

Fix $1\leq L<\infty$. The following conclusions hold uniformly for $c\in\mathcal S_L$ and for outgoing velocities off the axis.*



1.  *For every fixed $M,\delta>0$, $$\begin{aligned}
     &\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
     \sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}
     \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{\substack{|v|\leq M\\0<|\bar v|\leq\varepsilon}}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|\bar\xi|\geq\delta)=0,
     \label{ker:axis-input}\\
     &\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
     \sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}
     \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{\substack{|v|\leq M\\0<|\bar v|\leq\varepsilon}}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|v_3-p_3|\geq\delta)=0.
     \label{ker:axis-mismatch}
    \end{aligned}$$*



2.  *The axial input marginals are uniformly non-atomic. More precisely, for each $R\geq1$ and each interval $I$ of length $0<\ell\leq1$, $$\label{ker:axial-interval}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|p|+|\xi-v|\leq R,\ \xi_3\in I)
     \leq C_{q,m,L,R}\bigl(\ell+\ell^{2-q}\bigr).$$ In combination with <a href="#ker:uniform-tail" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ker:uniform-tail">[ker:uniform-tail]</a>, this gives $$\label{ker:axial-modulus}
     \lim_{\ell\downarrow0}\sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L}\mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_v
     \sup_{|I|\leq\ell}\mathcal K_c(v;\xi_3\in I)=0.$$*


3.  *Let $\mathcal S_L(\varepsilon)$ denote the subclass whose elementary transverse widths are at most $\varepsilon$. For every $M<\infty$ and $r_0>0$, $$\label{ker:off-axis-small-input}
     \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}\limsup_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}
     \sup_{c\in\mathcal S_L(\varepsilon)}
     \mathop{\mathrm{ess\,sup}}_{\substack{|v|\leq M\\|\bar v|\geq r_0}}
     \mathcal K_c(v;|\bar\xi|<\delta)=0.$$*

*All these conclusions hold under uniformly bounded sheet amplitudes.

On every compact set with $t\ge a>0$, the following statements hold uniformly.*


1.  *If $a\le t_2\le t_1$, $\Delta=t_1-t_2$, and $z_2=z_1-\Delta w$, then $$\label{nc:matching-inequality}
     |w-u_{\varrho,\sigma}(t_1,z_1)|
     \le |w-u_{\varrho,\sigma}(t_2,z_2)|.$$*


2.  *As $\varrho\downarrow0$, the distance of $u_{\varrho,\sigma}(t,z)$ to $$\{z/t,\ \sigma-\alpha,\ \sigma+\alpha\}$$ tends to zero uniformly. For the perturbed axial velocity in <a href="#nc:core-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="nc:core-parameters">[nc:core-parameters]</a>, $$\label{nc:thermal-speed-error}
     |p_3-u_{\varrho,\sigma}(t,z)|\le d_s|w_3|.$$

Let $n\ne0$, $e=n/|n|$, and let $\zeta$ be a bounded nonnegative function supported in $[-1,1]$ with integral one. For every nonnegative measurable $F$, $$\label{nc:plane-identity}
 \int_{n^\perp} F(k)\frac{\,\mathrm dA(k)}{|n|}
 =
 \int_{\mathbb R^3}F(n\times L)|n|\zeta(L\cdot e)\,\mathrm dL .$$ When $\delta\le|n|\le R$ and the plane integral is restricted to $|k|\le R_1$, the corresponding parameter measure $\mathbf 1_{\{|n\times L|\le R_1\}}|n|\zeta(L\cdot e)\,\mathrm dL$ is supported in $$|L|\le\sqrt{(R_1/\delta)^2+1}$$ and has density at most $R\|\zeta\|_\infty$. This bound concerns the parameter measure before multiplication by $F(n\times L)$. The identity can be applied successively when each normal is a measurable function of parameters chosen at earlier gains.

For these fixed $\varkappa,L$, there are $J_{\mathrm{br}}\in\mathbb N$ and $\varepsilon_*\in(0,1/4)$ with the following property. Suppose a measurable medium obeys, throughout every speed-$D$ excursion from a specified starting point over a duration $A\in I_L$, $$\label{br:medium-closeness}
|b_\sigma-1|\le\varepsilon_*,
\qquad |w_\sigma-\sigma e_3|\le\varepsilon_*
\quad(\sigma=\pm1).$$ From each starting velocity in $E$, histories with between $1$ and $J_{\mathrm{br}}$ collisions, speed at most $D$ on every free flight, every postcollision velocity and the final velocity strictly inside $E$, and $$\label{br:robust-drift}
|Z_A-\varkappa Ae_1|<d\varkappa A$$ have total weight greater than 3. The initial free flight may have speed $D$. No continuity of the medium is needed.

For the parameters just fixed, let $a_{\rm br}=L\varkappa/(\theta K)$. There is a time $t_{\mathrm{br}}>0$, independent of the mesh and $l\in[0,1]$, such that every density satisfying the equation, cap, and mild formula of Proposition <a href="#prop:finite-dilated-horizon" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:finite-dilated-horizon">44</a> on an interval containing $[t,(1+a_{\rm br})t]$, with $t\ge t_{\mathrm{br}}$, obeys, at its positive-time traces, $$\label{br:mass-growth}
 M((1+a_{\rm br})t)\ge3M(t),\qquad
 M(s):=\int_{\mathcal B_s}g(s,x,v)\,\mathrm dx\,\mathrm dv.$$
